course: dictionary
lesson: 4520
title: Headless: more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word headless is built from two parts: head and -less. The ending -less means without, so headless means having no head. «The headless horseman is an old legend, made famous in a story by Washington Irving in 1820.» The story is about a ghost, so the meaning is the simple one.
explain: Sometimes a word is used in a special way. «He ran around like a headless chicken before the test.» Nobody has lost their head here. The phrase is an informal idiom, and it means rushing about in a panic without thinking. Ask yourself: could this sentence be literally true? If not, the words may be doing something special.
explain: In computing, headless means something different again. A headless computer has no screen, and another computer controls it. The topic of the sentence tells you which meaning to use: a story about ghosts, a sentence about feelings, or a sentence about machines. Check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence.
fact: Headless means having no head.
fact: The ending -less means without, so careless means without care.
fact: Running around like a headless chicken is an informal idiom meaning rushing about in a panic without thinking.
fact: In computing, a headless computer has no screen and is controlled by another computer.
fact: The ostrich myth probably grew from what people saw from far away: an ostrich lowers its head to turn its eggs, so from a distance it can look headless.
exercise: «The headless horseman is an old legend.» What does headless mean here? | Riding a very fast horse | *Having no head | Very angry | Wearing a big hat
exercise: «He ran around like a headless chicken before the test.» What does this phrase mean? | Walking slowly and calmly | *Rushing about in a panic without thinking | Looking for a lost bird | Cooking dinner for everyone
exercise: «A headless computer has no screen.» What else is true of a headless computer? | *It is controlled by another computer | It has two screens | It is made of wood | It can talk to you
test: The word headless is made of head and -less. What does -less mean? | Under | Full of | *Without | Again
test: «The ostrich looks headless from far away.» Why might it look headless? | It has lost its head | It has three legs | It is always asleep | *Its head is hidden low, near the ground
exam: «Each myth usually hides something true.» What does this suggest about a myth? | It is always a complete lie | *It can contain a grain of truth inside it | It is a kind of computer | It can only be found in science books
